### 6. Performance Considerations

#### Mutex scalability

`std.Thread.Mutex` on Linux defaults to a `futex`‑based lock with no fairness guarantees. Under low contention it’s extremely fast: a lock/unlock pair is a few atomic operations. Under high contention, threads deschedule themselves via `FUTEX_WAIT`, which involves a context switch. Profiling with `perf` will show kernel time spent in `futex`.

For read‑heavy workloads, consider building a read‑write lock using a mutex and condition, or a specialized lock‑free structure. Zig’s standard library does not yet ship an `RwLock` (though one may be added). You can use `std.atomic.Value` for simple cases.

#### Condition variable overhead

On Linux, a `pthread_cond_signal`/`broadcast` requires a system call (unless the waiter list is empty, in which case the kernel can optimize). The mutex is released and reacquired inside the `wait` call, adding further context‑switch potential. For high‑throughput signaling, consider using a lock‑free SPSC queue built on atomics.

#### `ResetEvent` on Linux uses futex

The `ResetEvent` implementation is essentially a futex word with a manual‑reset semantic. `set()` writes a value and wakes all waiters with `FUTEX_WAKE`. `wait()` uses `FUTEX_WAIT` if the value indicates the event is not set. This is very lightweight, but broadcasting to many waiters can thundering‑herd the threads, all of which will contend on whatever shared resource they protect after waking.

#### Lock‑free queue performance

`std.atomic.Queue` (Michael‑Scott queue) has excellent scalability when producers and consumers are balanced. However, it is a linked‑list‑based queue, so each element requires a separate allocation, which can be a bottleneck. Throughput is typically limited by the allocator. The atomic CAS on the tail pointer can cause contention under many producers. For many‑producer/many‑consumer scenarios with high throughput, consider a bounded array‑based queue (ring buffer) with atomics.

#### Detecting races with ThreadSanitizer

Zig supports ThreadSanitizer (TSan) via `-fsanitize-thread`. When enabled, every memory access is instrumented to detect data races. This slows execution (2x–5x) and increases memory usage, but catches races that would otherwise slip into production. To enable:

```zig
// In build.zig:
exe.linkLibC();
exe.sanitize_thread = true;
```

During testing and CI, run your concurrent tests with TSan to detect unprotected access to shared mutable state.

